# i18n Function Selection

Translations are untrusted input: a translation file can contain markup. Escape
every translated string that is printed, using the escaping variant that matches
the output context. PHPCS (`WordPress.Security.EscapeOutput`) enforces this.

```php
// BAD - prints the translation unescaped
_e( 'Next Recipe', 'cooked' );
echo __( 'Next Recipe', 'cooked' );

// GOOD - HTML text
esc_html_e( 'Next Recipe', 'cooked' );
echo esc_html__( 'Next Recipe', 'cooked' );

// GOOD - attribute
<input placeholder="<?php esc_attr_e( 'Search recipes', 'cooked' ); ?>">

// GOOD - placeholder: translate, fill in, then escape the result once
echo esc_html( sprintf( __( 'Recipe by %s', 'cooked' ), $author_name ) );

// GOOD - translation that contains markup
echo wp_kses_post( __( 'Read the <a href="https://github.com/XjSv/Cooked/wiki">docs</a>.', 'cooked' ) );
```

Use plain `__()` only when the string is not printed directly: passed to a
function that escapes it, stored, or returned to a caller that escapes it at
output.

The text domain is `cooked` in Cooked and `cooked-pro` in Cooked Pro.
